1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,360
Two metres under a lake.

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:03,560
A pier above, a girl in a red

3
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:04,360
T-shirt below.

4
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:05,360
One person.

5
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:06,220
Eyes open.

6
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:07,120
Mouth open.

7
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:09,280
In the corner of the frame, a stopwatch

8
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:09,660
starts.

9
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:10,880
Ten seconds.

10
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:12,160
Thirty.

11
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:12,500
A minute.

12
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:14,320
She is not holding her breath.

13
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:15,500
She is breathing.

14
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:16,900
The water goes in.

15
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:18,040
Something comes out.

16
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:21,160
Her chest rises and falls like anyone's on

17
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:21,600
dry land.

18
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:23,800
Nobody on the pier knows how.

19
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:25,700
The answer is not in her lungs.

20
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:26,800
It is in a number.

21
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:28,440
Two hundred and fifty.

22
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:31,340
This is the story of one rule.

23
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:33,300
A person who can breathe water.

24
00:00:33,880 --> 00:00:35,380
Of a litre of air and a litre

25
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:35,780
of lake.

26
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:37,460
And the gap between them.

27
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:39,980
Of a fish that solves the problem with

28
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:40,600
a plumbing trick.

29
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:42,540
And a lung that was never built for

30
00:00:42,540 --> 00:00:42,940
it.

31
00:00:43,260 --> 00:00:45,820
Of mice in a tank in the nineteen-sixties,

32
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:48,560
and premature babies in a hospital in Philadelphia.

33
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:51,040
And an empty pier at dusk.

34
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:53,080
Could a human really do it?

35
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:55,580
And what would it cost her, minute by

36
00:00:55,580 --> 00:00:55,880
minute?

37
00:00:56,700 --> 00:00:59,500
This film is built on physiology textbooks, diving

38
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:02,480
manuals, clinical reviews and old laboratory reports.

39
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:03,960
It is a calculation,

40
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:05,140
not a prediction.

41
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:06,800
And the unknowns are many.

42
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:09,920
Every number comes from a named source, or

43
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:10,900
from arithmetic we show.

44
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:13,440
Where the sources disagree, we say so.

45
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:14,920
Start with the number.

46
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:16,080
Then the water.

47
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:17,300
Then the fish.

48
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:18,500
Then the liquid.

49
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:21,080
First, what a body needs.

50
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:23,564
At rest, an adult uses about two hundred

51
00:01:23,564 --> 00:01:25,280
and fifty millilitres of oxygen a minute.

52
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:27,860
That is the figure in standard physiology teaching.

53
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:30,784
One medical exam-prep page gets there from three

54
00:01:30,784 --> 00:01:33,060
and a half millilitres per kilogram, for a

55
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:33,980
seventy-kilogram adult.

56
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:36,090
About two hundred and forty-five.

57
00:01:36,090 --> 00:01:37,340
Two hundred and fifty.

58
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:37,940
Hold on to that.

59
00:01:38,580 --> 00:01:39,960
In air, it is easy to supply.

60
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:41,875
A litre of dry air is about twenty

61
00:01:41,875 --> 00:01:44,240
point nine percent oxygen, according to the United

62
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:46,140
Nations food agency's fisheries manual.

63
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:48,220
That is about two hundred and nine millilitres

64
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:49,680
in every litre.

65
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:52,040
Resting breathing moves about five to eight litres

66
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:53,620
of air a minute, according to the standard

67
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:54,960
reference on minute ventilation.

68
00:01:55,640 --> 00:01:56,100
Call it six.

69
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:57,460
Six litres of air.

70
00:01:57,460 --> 00:01:59,360
Two hundred and fifty millilitres of oxygen.

71
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:01,480
A comfortable, quiet job.

72
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:04,520
That is the deal the lungs were built

73
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:04,840
around.

74
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:06,240
Now change the fluid.

75
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:08,560
Water holds oxygen too.

76
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:10,480
Not much, says the sensor maker.

77
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:14,960
A manufacturer of dissolved-oxygen sensors says air-saturated water

78
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:17,900
at twenty-five degrees holds about eight point three

79
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:20,240
milligrams of oxygen per litre.

80
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:22,440
Convert that, and it is about five point

81
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:23,460
eight millilitres.

82
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:25,920
Our conversion, using a standard value for oxygen's

83
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:26,300
density.

84
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:28,300
Two hundred and nine in air.

85
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:29,980
Five point eight in water.

86
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:32,060
About thirty-six times less.

87
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:33,700
Colder water holds more.

88
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:35,000
Warm water holds less.

89
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:37,020
But the order of magnitude stays.

90
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:39,800
So how much water does she need?

91
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:42,605
If her lungs took every last molecule, two

92
00:02:42,605 --> 00:02:44,820
hundred and fifty divided by five point eight

93
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:46,360
is forty-three litres a minute.

94
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:47,900
That is the floor.

95
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:49,520
Physics will not let it go lower.

96
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:51,580
But lungs do not take every molecule.

97
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:54,500
An arXiv paper on lung ventilation in mammals

98
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:57,115
reports that exhaled air is sixteen to eighteen

99
00:02:57,115 --> 00:02:59,300
percent oxygen, when the air going in is

100
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:02,960
twenty-one.

101
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:04,420
A drop of three to five points.

102
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:06,480
That is roughly a seventh to a quarter

103
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:07,280
of what comes in.

104
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:08,300
Our arithmetic.

105
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:09,920
Take the generous end.

106
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:10,680
A quarter.

107
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:14,260
Two hundred and fifty, divided by five point

108
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:16,080
eight, divided by a quarter.

109
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:18,500
One hundred and seventy-two litres a minute.

110
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:20,680
At the stingy end, it is more than

111
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:22,440
three hundred.

112
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:23,940
How much can a person breathe?

113
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:27,160
A physiology module at Monash University gives a

114
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:30,204
mean maximum voluntary ventilation of about a hundred

115
00:03:30,204 --> 00:03:31,980
and forty litres a minute for healthy young

116
00:03:31,980 --> 00:03:32,440
men.

117
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:34,820
Range, a hundred to a hundred and eighty.

118
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:36,420
For women, about a hundred.

119
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:38,280
Seventy to a hundred and twenty.

120
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:40,200
That is a short test, the module says,

121
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:42,240
with the subject trying as hard as possible.

122
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:44,440
Her resting need is higher than that.

123
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:45,320
At rest.

124
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:48,140
Then there is what water is.

125
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:50,700
It is about eight hundred times denser than

126
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:50,920
air.

127
00:03:51,580 --> 00:03:53,440
Course notes give a ratio of about eight

128
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:55,440
hundred and thirty, a flashcard set says.

129
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:58,260
One litre of water weighs one kilogram.

130
00:03:58,260 --> 00:04:00,660
A hundred and seventy-two litres a minute is

131
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:02,100
a hundred and seventy-two kilograms.

132
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:03,420
Through the throat.

133
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:04,720
Every minute.

134
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:05,900
While resting.

135
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:08,600
And the lung has a second problem.

136
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:10,680
It breathes in and out through the same

137
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:10,960
pipes.

138
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:14,300
A textbook puts the anatomical dead space at

139
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:16,400
about a hundred and fifty millilitres, out of

140
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:18,300
a resting breath of five hundred.

141
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:20,660
That is the volume of the airways themselves.

142
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:22,720
Whatever sits there at the end of an

143
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:23,620
outbreath is stale.

144
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:25,620
The next breath mixes with it.

145
00:04:26,020 --> 00:04:27,400
In air, that costs a little.

146
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:30,040
The textbook says it leaves the oxygen pressure

147
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:31,740
in the lung lower than in the air

148
00:04:31,740 --> 00:04:32,200
outside.

149
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:34,340
In water, count it.

150
00:04:34,340 --> 00:04:36,060
Five hundred millilitres in.

151
00:04:36,060 --> 00:04:37,700
A hundred and fifty of it stale.

152
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:40,040
Three hundred and fifty reach the air sacs.

153
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:42,540
To move a hundred and seventy-two litres of

154
00:04:42,540 --> 00:04:44,790
fresh water through them, that is nearly five

155
00:04:44,790 --> 00:04:46,040
hundred breaths a minute.

156
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:48,980
Or take the biggest breath she has.

157
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:52,060
Total lung capacity is about six litres, and

158
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:54,480
the usual vital capacity is four point six

159
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:56,440
to four point eight, depending on the source.

160
00:04:56,440 --> 00:04:58,540
A full breath every time.

161
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:00,120
Nearly forty a minute.

162
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:02,400
Each one about four and a half kilograms

163
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:02,880
of lake.

164
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:04,080
Our arithmetic.

165
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:06,140
Neither is a pace a person holds.

166
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:08,360
No source we read says anyone has.

167
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:10,620
All this is hypothetical.

168
00:05:10,620 --> 00:05:12,740
Here is what happens in the real world.

169
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:14,340
When someone inhales water,

170
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:17,340
a clinical update on drowning describes two paths.

171
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:19,920
In roughly ten to twenty percent of victims,

172
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:21,260
the throat clamps shut.

173
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:22,320
Laryngospasm.

174
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:25,700
The airway closes and the person asphyxiates, with

175
00:05:25,700 --> 00:05:26,960
little water reaching the lungs.

176
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:29,940
Another source puts it lower, under ten to

177
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:31,240
fifteen.

178
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:33,860
In most, the throat relaxes and water goes

179
00:05:33,860 --> 00:05:34,160
in.

180
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:37,680
Victims typically aspirate about two to four millilitres

181
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:38,440
per kilogram,

182
00:05:39,220 --> 00:05:40,000
according to the same reviews.

183
00:05:40,780 --> 00:05:41,560
A small volume.

184
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:42,980
But it does damage.

185
00:05:43,740 --> 00:05:45,440
The lungs line their air sacs with a

186
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:46,860
thin film called surfactant.

187
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:48,040
It keeps them open.

188
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:49,740
Water disrupts it.

189
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:50,840
The sacs collapse.

190
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:52,980
Oxygen can no longer cross.

191
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:56,100
Older teaching said fresh water and salt water

192
00:05:56,100 --> 00:05:57,280
injure the lungs differently.

193
00:05:58,040 --> 00:06:00,120
Current clinical sources say the practical difference is

194
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:00,320
minor.

195
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:02,380
The result is the same chain.

196
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:07,100
Collapse, falling oxygen, rising carbon dioxide, and then

197
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:07,420
the heart.

198
00:06:08,420 --> 00:06:10,480
So our hero's lungs would need two things.

199
00:06:11,180 --> 00:06:12,280
A way to avoid the damage.

200
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:14,420
And a way to move far more water

201
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:15,580
than any lung has moved.

202
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:16,760
Remember the second.

203
00:06:18,180 --> 00:06:19,040
Fish do it.

204
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:19,920
Every minute.

205
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:20,780
How?

206
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:21,860
Two tricks.

207
00:06:21,860 --> 00:06:23,260
One.

208
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:25,160
Their gills run blood and water in opposite

209
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:25,720
directions.

210
00:06:26,300 --> 00:06:28,800
The most oxygen-rich blood meets the most oxygen-rich

211
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:29,180
water.

212
00:06:29,640 --> 00:06:30,820
The least meets the least.

213
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:33,240
Diffusion keeps working along the whole surface.

214
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:34,680
How well does it work?

215
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:35,760
Sources differ.

216
00:06:36,280 --> 00:06:38,240
A textbook says up to eighty-five percent of

217
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:39,180
the oxygen in the water.

218
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:41,600
A school resource says about eighty.

219
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:43,440
The Natural History Museum in London says up

220
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:44,480
to seventy.

221
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:46,940
An animal-physiology text says ninety or more is

222
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:47,280
possible.

223
00:06:47,780 --> 00:06:48,276
Take eighty.

224
00:06:48,276 --> 00:06:51,167
Two hundred and fifty, divided by five point

225
00:06:51,167 --> 00:06:52,960
eight, divided by point eight.

226
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:54,340
About fifty-four litres a minute.

227
00:06:54,760 --> 00:06:57,460
Not one hundred and seventy-two.

228
00:06:57,460 --> 00:06:58,060
Two.

229
00:06:58,060 --> 00:06:59,180
The water flows one way.

230
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:00,940
It goes in the mouth, over the gills,

231
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:01,580
out the side.

232
00:07:02,060 --> 00:07:02,700
No dead space.

233
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:04,960
No stale pool in the airways.

234
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:07,880
A physiology text says fish greatly reduce the

235
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:10,120
energy cost of moving water by keeping that

236
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:11,880
flow continuous and in one direction.

237
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:14,300
A lung does the opposite.

238
00:07:14,860 --> 00:07:16,220
It is a dead-end sac.

239
00:07:16,220 --> 00:07:17,920
The same pipe in, the same pipe out.

240
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:19,960
So there is a first way to make

241
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:20,640
her breathing work.

242
00:07:21,240 --> 00:07:21,720
Change her.

243
00:07:22,220 --> 00:07:22,840
Give her gills.

244
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:24,720
Or something with gills' efficiency.

245
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:26,000
A one-way flow.

246
00:07:26,340 --> 00:07:27,400
A far better extraction.

247
00:07:28,160 --> 00:07:29,320
That is a different animal.

248
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:31,580
Nothing in this film says it is impossible.

249
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:33,360
It is not a lung anymore.

250
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:36,080
Whether any human body could be rebuilt that

251
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:37,200
way is not known.

252
00:07:38,100 --> 00:07:41,080
Duke University, early nineteen-sixties.

253
00:07:41,080 --> 00:07:43,960
According to a Gizmodo history, Johannes Kylstra's team

254
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:46,740
trained mice to breathe saline loaded with oxygen

255
00:07:46,740 --> 00:07:48,280
under a pressure of a hundred and sixty

256
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:49,040
atmospheres.

257
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:50,740
The mice died minutes later.

258
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:52,840
Carbon dioxide had built up.

259
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:55,720
Another account says rodents and even dogs lasted

260
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:56,680
up to about an hour.

261
00:07:57,560 --> 00:07:58,380
Accounts differ on the time.

262
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:00,060
They agree on the cause.

263
00:08:00,840 --> 00:08:02,360
The saline delivered enough oxygen.

264
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:04,780
It did not clear the carbon dioxide fast

265
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:05,100
enough.

266
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:09,060
The same fisheries manual says carbon dioxide is

267
00:08:09,060 --> 00:08:11,520
far more soluble in water than oxygen is.

268
00:08:11,720 --> 00:08:13,680
Which is one reason gills shed it easily.

269
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:16,580
But a saline-filled lung is not a gill.

270
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:17,680
Our reading:

271
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:20,260
it is the same dead-end sac, filled with

272
00:08:20,260 --> 00:08:21,500
a liquid that barely moves.

273
00:08:22,140 --> 00:08:23,580
The sources do not test that.

274
00:08:23,580 --> 00:08:26,020
Mid-nineteen-sixties.

275
00:08:26,020 --> 00:08:27,580
Clark and Gollan.

276
00:08:27,580 --> 00:08:28,540
They switched the liquid.

277
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:29,400
Not water.

278
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:31,160
A perfluorocarbon.

279
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:33,420
A fluid that dissolves both gases.

280
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:36,500
Gizmodo says their mice breathed it at normal

281
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:36,960
pressure.

282
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:39,200
Rats stayed under for up to twenty hours.

283
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:40,500
Cats, for weeks.

284
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:43,980
An older encyclopedia mirror adds one detail that

285
